---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Single-location dental practice, Veneto' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Initiative in 2026, part of an ongoing relationship since 2023' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Strategic discovery, copywriting, post-lead sales protocol, campaign management, reporting' },
  { label: 'Channel', value: 'Meta Ads' },
  { label: 'Team', value: 'Practice owner (clinical input), front desk (protocol execution)' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '8.53×', label: 'ROAS on ad spend' },
  { metric: '83.3%', label: 'close rate after the visit was completed' },
  { metric: '23.8%', label: 'from contact to closed treatment' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Building a specialist positioning, from discovery to closing the quote"
  description="Case study: clinical-communication positioning for a complex service, with discovery, copywriting and a sales protocol. 8.53x ROAS."
  eyebrow="Strategy · Copywriting · Funnel"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>
    Periodontitis is a serious, widespread disease that's almost invisible to the people who have
    it: it doesn't hurt at first, and its signs are systematically underestimated. For many
    practices it's easier to offer an implant than to save the tooth. The owner wanted the
    opposite positioning: becoming the local reference point for patients who want to save their
    teeth.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Making a condition the patient doesn't perceive feel tangible and urgent, without alarmism and
    while respecting the rules on healthcare communication — and building a post-lead journey able
    to turn clinical understanding into a treatment decision.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    I personally ran the discovery with the practice owner: a structured interview to extract the
    target audience, overlooked clinical signals, recurring objections, tone of voice. From that
    material I built the communication framework (six distinct angles, from "35 years old and
    already being offered implants?" to the cost comparison between treatment and implant) and
    wrote the eight campaign copy variants. I then defined, together with the front desk, the
    post-lead protocol: what to say on the phone, what must always happen at the first visit, how
    to present the treatment plan.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    The funnel immediately revealed where it wins and where it loses: almost one contact in two
    books a visit (47.6%), and whoever makes it to the practice closes treatment in 83.3% of
    cases. The weak point is in the middle: only 60% of booked visits are actually attended. The
    problem wasn't lead quality, but the in-practice presentation stage.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    Open point: we've worked on the show-up rate through more consistent contact with the front
    desk, but at the time of writing there's no new data yet measuring its effect. I'd rather
    state that than claim an unverified improvement.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution</h2>
  <p>
    Eight copy variants within a consistent creative territory, tested on Meta. A phone script for
    the front desk built to communicate value before price. A standardized first visit — exams,
    photographic documentation, clinical record — designed to let the patient "see" their own
    situation, turning the diagnosis into a moment of awareness rather than a quote imposed on
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    42 contacts produced 20 booked visits, 12 attended and 10 closed treatments, for €18,845 in
    accepted value against €2,208.34 in ad spend: a ROAS of 8.53×. Almost one contact in four
    (23.8%) made it all the way to closed treatment.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    That a complex clinical insight can become a repeatable communication framework, if the
    discovery is done with method. And that the post-lead funnel has to be designed together with
    whoever runs it every day: the most effective script is the one the front desk makes their
    own, not one imposed from outside.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
